6. Why TSMC's moat is partly a yield moat

TSMC buys equipment from many of the same major vendors available to Samsung and Intel: ASML, Applied Materials, Lam Research, KLA, Tokyo Electron and others.

The moat is not simply owning machines.

TSMC's advantage includes process integration: knowing how to combine thousands of steps, recipes, materials and tool settings into high-volume production with commercially attractive yield.

A process that works in a laboratory is not the same as a process that reliably produces millions of chips.

This is why a competitor cannot recreate TSMC merely by buying equivalent equipment.

Manufacturing learning matters.

7. The yield learning curve

When a new process node launches, yield is usually worse than it will be later.

Engineers then improve it over time.

The basic cycle is:

Run wafers → inspect defects → identify root cause → adjust process recipe or tool → run more wafers → measure again.

Every cycle produces information.

Over time, the fab learns which defects matter, where variation originates and how to keep each step within tighter tolerances.

This is called yield learning.

A faster yield-learning curve means a company can reach profitable high-volume manufacturing sooner.

8. Why early yield is strategically important

Suppose two foundries launch competing advanced nodes.

Both can technically manufacture the chip.

Foundry A reaches 80% yield relatively quickly.

Foundry B remains at 50%.

Foundry A can offer customers:

- lower effective cost per good die;
- more predictable supply;
- better delivery schedules;
- more confidence for large-volume product launches.

Foundry B may need to charge less, absorb lower margins, delay production ramps or spend more time fixing the process.

So yield can turn into customer share.

And customer share creates more wafer volume, which creates more manufacturing data, which can improve yield further.

That can become a reinforcing loop.

9. The learning flywheel

A simplified foundry flywheel is:

More leading-edge customers → more wafer starts → more manufacturing data → faster defect learning → better yield → stronger customer economics → more leading-edge customers.

This is one reason scale can create a moat in advanced semiconductor manufacturing.

The important insight is that volume is not only revenue.

Volume can also be learning.

A smaller competitor may have technically capable equipment but fewer opportunities to discover rare failure modes at production scale.

10. Why process control companies benefit

Yesterday we covered KLA.

Yield explains why customers will spend heavily on inspection and metrology.

A process-control tool is valuable if it helps the fab find a defect source before thousands of additional wafers are affected.

Suppose a tool identifies a subtle lithography overlay problem one day earlier.

That may prevent millions of dollars of bad work-in-process from moving through downstream equipment.

So the economic value of process control is not measured only by the price of the inspection tool.

It is measured partly by the yield loss it prevents.

This helps explain why KLA can have strong economics even though its machines do not directly create the transistor.

